# DISABLED — deliberately, and not because it is broken.
#
# GitHub only reads `.yml` and `.yaml` in this directory, so the `.disabled`
# suffix is what switches this off. The file is kept rather than deleted
# because the work in it is fine; it is the *decision* behind it that is not
# made yet.
#
# Why it is off:
#
#   This workflow would publish `path: '.'` — the whole repository as a
#   website: the old vanilla portal at the root as the home page, plus docs/,
#   tests/, scripts/ and web/ source. It has never actually published
#   anything. Pages was never enabled on this repository, there have been zero
#   deployments in its lifetime, and the workflow ran exactly once, on the
#   merge of pull request #1, where the test job failed ahead of the deploy.
#   So nobody has ever been served anything by it.
#
#   With the test job fixed, the deploy job would have run and then failed at
#   `actions/configure-pages` — `enablement` defaults to `false` and
#   `GITHUB_TOKEN` cannot enable Pages. That is a `main` permanently red on a
#   job nobody intends to fix this week, which is how a team learns to ignore a
#   red `main`. Deferring the question consciously is the point; leaving a
#   standing failure as a reminder is not.
#
# What has to be true before it comes back:
#
#   1. A real exams section, not a stub — the first of the nine parity
#      conditions in docs/07-claim-sweep.md.
#   2. An About / independence notice as its own destination — the third.
#      Those are the two that are unmet, and they are why `app/` cannot be
#      deleted yet.
#   3. A decision taken knowingly, because **publishing `web/dist` IS the
#      decision to retire `app/`.** The published directory is not a
#      deployment detail: whichever one is served is the product. Switching it
#      retires the vanilla portal as the live site, and that is exactly what
#      the nine conditions exist to gate.
#
#   And whichever is chosen, this workflow needs a build step. It has none, and
#   `web/dist` does not exist on a fresh runner. The premise it was written
#   under — "the repository is the website, no build step" — was true of the
#   vanilla portal and is false now. See docs/deploying.md.

name: Deploy to GitHub Pages

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  workflow_dispatch:

permissions:
  contents: read
  pages: write
  id-token: write

# A queued deploy replaces a waiting one; a deploy already running is left to
# finish rather than cancelled half-published.
concurrency:
  group: pages
  cancel-in-progress: false

jobs:
  # Named `pages-test`, not `test`. The ruleset requires status checks called
  # `test` and `browser`, which are ci.yml's jobs; a second job also called
  # `test` makes the required check ambiguous — two different runs reporting
  # under one name, and whichever lands last is what the branch appears to say.
  pages-test:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: '22'
      # The resolver hook, which ci.yml has had since the suite was pointed at
      # web/. Without it Node cannot resolve a relative import of a .ts module
      # and every test that imports one dies on load. This workflow only runs on
      # a push to main, and the resolver only reached main when PR #1 merged —
      # so it was latently broken for as long as it existed and fired the first
      # time it was ever exercised. A job that cannot run on the branch it
      # guards is a job nobody has seen.
      - run: node --import ./tests/register.mjs --test tests/*.test.js

  deploy:
    needs: pages-test    # never publish a build whose tests did not pass
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    environment:
      name: github-pages
      url: ${{ steps.deployment.outputs.page_url }}
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4

      # There is no build step. The repository is the site: static files only,
      # no bundler, no dependencies, nothing to compile.
      - uses: actions/configure-pages@v5
      - uses: actions/upload-pages-artifact@v3
        with:
          path: '.'
      - id: deployment
        uses: actions/deploy-pages@v4
